      "synopsis": "This course develops the analytical and leadership capabilities required to navigate politically and socially complex environments, with a particular focus on emerging markets. These contexts often combine high uncertainty, gaps in market-supporting institutions, and significant opportunities for value creation. Students learn to diagnose sociopolitical risks, identify key stakeholders, and design strategies that balance financial, operational, and legitimacy considerations. Through cases, debates, and an immersive crisis simulation, the course builds practical judgment for operating across sectors—including governments, NGOs, and communities—and equips students to design sustainable, resilient strategies that align organizational goals with societal expectations. At the end of the course, students will be able to:\n1. Analyze external and internal sociopolitical factors shaping firm strategy in emerging markets using stakeholder- and risk-mapping tools.\n2. Evaluate stakeholder interests, power dynamics, and likely reactions to anticipate political and social risks under conditions of uncertainty.\n3. Design strategic responses that integrate participatory engagement, cross-sector collaboration, and sustainability principles into organizational decision-making.",
