      "synopsis": "This course provides an introduction to the strategic management of technology, especially with respect to platform strategy and artificial intelligence.",
      "synopsisHeading": "OVERVIEW",
      "synopsisPage": 46,
      "learningOutcomes": "Course organization – detailed outline The course has three goals, which relate to the three modules of the course. The purpose of the first module, “Technology- based Competitive Advantage,” is to understand how technology and digitization affect firms’ profitability and growth. In the second module, “Technology Platforms and Network Dynamics,” we will take a deep-dive on one of the most successful and lucrative business models to emerge over the past thirty years: the multi-sided platform. We will examine how firms utilize technology to harness network effects, use matching mechanisms to solve economic and social problems, and gain competitive advantage and capture value by doing so. In our final module, “Platforms & AI in Strategy and Society,” we will explore how technology can solve some social and business problems but can also create ethical dilemmas. Specific topics and questions include but are not limited to the following:\n• How should an incumbent firm respond to “disruptive” innovations?\n• How can a mechanism design framework be used to design and analyze platform dynamics?\n• How do firms leverage network effects to build sustainable competitive advantage?\n• What are the optimal strategies for building multi-sided platforms?\n• How can data and AI capabilities be used in technology and platform strategy?\n• How do new technologies (especially AI) change the way we interact in society, and how should businesses respond? ESG-related Content Competency Goals Environmental- Social- Governance- Sustainable Transformative Innovative Thinking Positive related related related Business Management & Problem Solving Leadership content content content Practices\n1. Minimal 3. Significant 2. Moderate 1. Minimal 2. Moderate 1. Minimal 2. Moderate content content content contribution contribution contribution contribution TEACHING MATERIALS All cases, required readings, and videos will be made available via links in the Calendar section of the course website. There is no physical casebook for this course. There are a few optional books that you might find helpful as well. OPTIONAL, BUT HIGHLY RECOMMENDED READINGS\n• D’Aveni, Richard. 2018. The Pan-Industrial Revolution: How New Manufacturing Titans Will Transform the World. Houghton Mifflin Harcourt.\n• Kearns, Michael and Aaron Roth. 2019. The Ethical Algorithm: The Science of Socially Aware Algorithm Design. Oxford University Press. Page 1 of 5\n• McAfee, Andrew and Erik Brynjoslfsson. 2017. Machine, Crowd, Platform: Harness- ing our Digital Future. WW Norton.\n• Stone, Brad. 2014. The Everything Store: Jeff Bezos and the Age of Amazon. Little, Brown and Company.\n• Stone, Brad. 2017. The Upstarts: How Uber Airbnb, and the Killer Companies of the New Silicon Valley are Changing the World. Little, Brown and Company.",
      "learningOutcomesHeading": "LEARNING OUTCOMES",
      "totalSessions": 16,
      "totalHours": 24.0
