      "synopsis": "Why do some organisations see opportunities that their competitors miss or fail to act on? Despite unprecedented amounts of data, businesses often struggle to understand people in context. Demographic, psychographic and behavioural segmentation can reveal useful patterns, but each isolates a narrow set of measurable traits and behaviours from the wider fabric that gives them meaning. A sports footwear company is not only competing in the footwear category; a new drug’s success does not depend only on whether it works for an identified medical condition; and new parents are not simply a target group. Companies and their products operate in worlds: evolving ecosystems of people, organisations, practices, technologies and institutions, held together by ideas, norms and shared assumptions. Understanding these worlds helps organisations see why behaviours cluster, which adjacent needs emerge and how behaviour may shift as a world evolves, enabling sharper insights, segmentation models and strategies. In this seminar, students will learn how ReD uses the social sciences and the Worlds approach to help clients make sharper business choices. They will be introduced to the main concepts and practices involved in applying social-science methods to business problems. Using contemporary cases, they will map a world, identify its dimensions, and explore how an organisation can participate credibly within it. Students will leave able to recognise when a Worlds approach is useful, apply it to a business problem and translate a richer understanding of human behaviour into a focused strategic hypothesis.",
      "synopsisHeading": "Synopsis / Overview",
      "synopsisPage": 17,
      "learningOutcomes": "At the end of the seminar, students will be able to:\n• Distinguish a world from a market, industry, category, community and consumer segment.\n• Apply social-science and human-centred approaches to research and analyse business problems.\n• Translate a world-level insight into a focused strategic hypothesis about where an organisation could play, what role it could adopt and how it might create value.",
      "learningOutcomesHeading": "Learning Outcomes",
      "totalSessions": 2,
      "totalHours": 3.0
